A Run by the Lake and Breakfast# Next morning, Anna woke up as the first light of day came in through her curtains. She wanted to seize the early morning for a quick run, so she got up and quickly dressed. She chose a tank top and leggings, comfortable and perfect for running. Then she put on her running shoes and a light jacket, because it would still be chilly by the lakeside. A headband kept her hair out of her face.
A few stretches and a quick warmup inside, and then she went out, heading down for the lake, turning north at the shore. She loved to run early in the morning, when the city was still quiet. Cool, wet air from the lake filled her lungs as she started on the path, with earthy fragrances from the park. The sun broke through the leaves of the trees. It was peaceful and beautiful, her favorite path at this time of the year.
She ran hard, and sped up more at intervals, until her muscles burned. When she felt sufficiently burned out, she turned around, keeping a brisk but steady pace. This, the way back, was the hard part, with the initial buoyancy of the early morning gone, powering through. She enjoyed that challenge. Heading home, she came out of the nature by the lake shore back into the city, which was now slowly waking up to an early Saturday morning.
Back at her apartment, she sloppily dropped her muddy shoes by the door, and stripped, leaving a line of discarded clothing behind her on the way to the shower. Turning it on and setting it to &ldquo;cook me slowly,&rdquo; she stepped in, washing off the sweat and dirt from her morning exercise. She felt good and ready for the day; the run had energized her. Still naked, she sat down at her desk, VPN&rsquo;ed into GAIA. and checked in again on the sequencers. Everything was okay and on schedule: The data would be ready in time. Just not today.
She then picked up fresh clothing, her usual &ldquo;runners casual&rdquo; and went out. Her steps led her down to the Lakeside Café, where she would have met Sonia today, and ordered her usual croissant and coffee. She sat away from the other patrons, enjoying her breakfast, and also trying to keep up at least some semblance of isolation out of an abundance of caution.
Her mind wandered to Sonia, the virus and her work. She had been putting on a good face yesterday to Sonia, but these CRISP-Fold markers worried her to no end. With the symptoms reported, the virus seemed harmless enough, but why go through all this trouble and construct a thing with such a giant genome, just to get a bad head cold? Was it badly constructed, the genome&rsquo;s size an artifact of the process? But with what little they saw last night, it did not look like this. It was elegant, advanced even, in a certain, intangible and hard to describe intuitive way that only an expert in viral genetics could see. It was designed, and with some purpose, and that purpose was inscrutable, certainly not a simple cold.
Sitting at her table and looking over the lake, Anna overheard a conversation at a nearby table. A young woman argued, full of frustration. She sat among colleagues, who did not take her ideas seriously. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s just not practical,&rdquo; said one, a man of course, dismissing her suggestions outright. Anna stopped eating and listened; she had heard this particular sentence far too often. The woman was apparently named Sienna, by her accent very British, and from what the group spoke about, all of them working at CERN here in Geneva. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve been analyzing the thermal and structural properties of these new composite materials,&rdquo; she reasoned. &ldquo;My simulations show a potential 20% increase in efficiency if we integrate them into our magnets&rsquo; next upgrade.&rdquo; And was shot down immediately. &ldquo;Well, Sienna, that sounds interesting,&rdquo; a German accent chimed in. &ldquo;But you know, these materials have never been tested under the extreme conditions we see in accelerators. It&rsquo;s quite a leap to assume they&rsquo;d just work without extensive validation,&rdquo; one of her male companions raised.
And the other jumped in, &ldquo;Exactly, Markus is right. You&rsquo;re making some big assumptions about material behavior here. I think you&rsquo;re not considering the practical engineering challenges. We&rsquo;ve been working with the current materials for decades because they are proven.&rdquo; Anna felt a surge of frustration turning into barely contained anger, with the resolve to remain composed. She had been in the exact same situation far too often, and felt drawn to the woman. She wanted to help, had to show her she wasn&rsquo;t alone in this. Finally, she resolved to jump in, got up, and walked over. She even managed to mostly keep the sharp tone out of her voice.
&ldquo;Excuse me, I&rsquo;m Anna. Mind if I jump in here? I&rsquo;ve been overhearing bits of your conversation, and, well, it&rsquo;s quite the déjà vu for me,&rdquo; she started, almost friendly. Sienna turned around and looked at her, up and down, then decided to introduce herself and her companions. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m Sienna, these are Markus and Luca. We&rsquo;re working at CERN. Please join us.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Thank you, Sienna,&rdquo; Anna started, and paused to collect herself. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s just&hellip; hearing about these new materials and then the immediate leap to skepticism? It&rsquo;s almost like a reflex, isn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo; Anna laughed softly, but the laughter did not reach her eyes. &ldquo;My mother, Elena Lefèvre, works in biomathematics, maybe you&rsquo;ve heard of her, she went through the same kind of… rigorous &lsquo;debate.&rsquo;&rdquo;
Markus nodded. &ldquo;Yes, Elena Lefèvre is quite renowned in her field.&rdquo;
Anna sighed: &ldquo;Right, renowned now, but not before she had to practically move mountains to get her ideas off the ground. But I suppose that&rsquo;s how it goes, isn&rsquo;t it? You propose something new, and it&rsquo;s met with—how should I put it?—a healthy dose of traditionalism.&rdquo;
Luca interjected: &ldquo;Well, it&rsquo;s important to be cautious with untested innovations, isn&rsquo;t it? I mean, our field is called High Energy Physics for a reason.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Absolutely, caution is key,&rdquo; Anna said with a thin smile. &ldquo;But so is progress, wouldn&rsquo;t you say? My mother once said, ‘To innovate is to invite dispute.&rsquo; She pushed for the use of novel computational models and, well, let&rsquo;s just say the initial response was less than enthusiastic.&rdquo; She paused, searching carefully for words. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s curious, though, how once the results were in, the same skeptics hailed these innovations as game-changers.&rdquo;
This is where Sienna picked up: &ldquo;That&rsquo;s exactly the spirit I want to bring to our work. Starting with a pilot project might allow us to demonstrate the practical benefits without overcommitting.&rdquo;
Anna lent her support: &ldquo;A pilot project sounds like a perfect compromise. It gives you a chance to test the waters without… well, sinking the ship with doubt before it even leaves the harbor.&rdquo; Finally, she managed a genuine smile. &ldquo;I think it&rsquo;s worth considering, don&rsquo;t you?&rdquo;
The trio went back to discussing Sienna&rsquo;s idea, this time taking her more seriously, and actually making constructive contributions. Anna slowly went back to her table, trying to get back into a relaxed breakfast mood again. A memory came to her of her mother Elena. Anna was only ten years old back then, and for the first time stayed with her at a conference, quietly listening and trying to understand what grownups do and how they speak.
It was at a conference on computational biology in Paris. Elena got involved in a discussion over lunch. Her sparring partner was Professor Martin Keller, a respected researcher with set ideas. They spoke about biomathematical models in understanding viral replication. That was a field where Elena had recently published a groundbreaking paper. Keller did not realize that the author was this Elena, and mansplained to her: &ldquo;You should really read Lefèvre et al. on the subject,&rdquo; he advised.
&ldquo;I am Lefèvre et al.,&rdquo; Elena corrected him, showing him her name tag. Within a laughing crowd, Keller was embarrassed but respected her more after that. For Anna, watching her mother stand up for herself was important. She was her role-model, she taught her to be strong and speak up. Anna smiled, remembering her mother&rsquo;s courage. She felt proud and ready to face her own challenges.

Sonia meets the Changed Anna# Anna and Sonia had planned to meet at their usual spot, the Lakeside Café. But that did not work out, because Sonia felt unwell after returning from Shenzhen, and the infection outbreak that followed and brought the Change to them. As Sonia arrived now, a good week later, the atmosphere at the café had been completely transformed. To her, it felt like a weird costume drama, evoking a sense of nostalgia that made her uneasy. The café was filled with the Changed—both women and men. Both looked as though they had stepped out of mid-twentieth-century fashion magazines. The men were all in tailored suits, crisp white shirts with black ties, some opting for a sporty casual look with cardigans over polo shirts and dress trousers. But it was the women who particularly caught her eye. They were all in dresses or skirts, with gloves and either hats or stylish headscarves, the faces with meticulously applied makeup, sporting small clutches.
Sonia navigated the crowd, searching for Anna. She felt like an outsider. Although she had attempted to adjust her look to match the look of the Changed, it was clear she wasn&rsquo;t wearing the &ldquo;uniform&rdquo; correctly. Dressed in casual 2020s office chic, with low heels, pantyhose, a knee-length skirt and blouse, and mild makeup, she tried to blend in. However, she quickly realized she was the only one without gloves and with uncovered hair, and was underdressed compared to everyone else. Some people looked at her with disdain, as if she was a filthy clochard or maybe half-naked.
When she finally spotted Anna, she barely recognized her. To Sonia, she looked like a complete stranger.
Anna&rsquo;s transformation was complete. Gone were the casual runner&rsquo;s chic and sneakers. Instead, she wore an A-line skirt that gained volume with pettcoats, and a richly embroidered Victorian-style button-up blouse with a high, tight collar brushing against her chin. White satin gloves covering her hands, and her hair was completely covered by a sand-colored hijab. She also wore sophisticated high heels—a stark departure from her usual sneakers— and her makeup was meticulously applied in a bold, defined 1950s style.
Sonia had recovered from her sickness late and only the day before, and found her body grotesquely transformed and manipulated by the Virus. Today, filling up her depleted fridge and later, on her way to the café, Sonia&rsquo;s unease had been accumulating. She had spotted the large number of Changed in the street, easily recognizable from the so far uninfected in dress and behavior. She knew she should also dress this way, the &ldquo;correct way,&rdquo; but easily ignored this ridiculous suggestion. Going down to the Lakeside Café, she saw larger numbers of Changed, and her unease mounted, accumulating as a simmering rage underneath the surface.
As she saw the Changed Anna, Sonia&rsquo;s shock unleashed her fury, when she realized that the grotesque alterations forced upon her own body and Anna&rsquo;s were just the surface; there was a vast, unseen force at work that was orchestrating a widespread transformation. An artificially engineered virus, a tool of subjugation, had been unleashed to mold her and countless others into grotesque parodies of womanhood. This was not just a physical change.
It was a sinister assault on their very essence, a calculated erasure of autonomy, programming their flesh and minds to fit someone else&rsquo;s twisted fantasy. Women were being reduced to mere objects, sculpted for the pleasure and scrutiny of the male gaze. The virus was designed to turn her and everyone else into this. Sonia&rsquo;s fury burned brightly. She was more than this reductive vision of femininity, more than a puppet manipulated by patriarchal designs. She was a woman, a sovereign entity, not an ornament to be passively admired.
Sonia stopped, and took a deep breath, trying to maintain her composure. She could not do much right now, and had to try to connect to Anna in order to better understand what had happened and why Anna and the other Changed acted the way they did. Apparently she herself was somehow different. &ldquo;Anna?&rdquo; Sonia&rsquo;s voice trembled slightly as she reached the table.
Anna looked up, her smile serene yet somehow distant. &ldquo;Sonia, it&rsquo;s wonderful to see you,&rdquo; she replied, her tone measured and more formal than Sonia was used to. They exchanged a brief, awkward hug—Anna&rsquo;s embrace was stiff, formal. Sonia pulled back, searching Anna&rsquo;s face for signs of the friend she knew. She took a seat opposite Anna, signaling a server to approach. &ldquo;Two coffees, please,&rdquo; Sonia ordered. The server nodded and moved away.
&ldquo;So,&rdquo; Sonia began, her eyes locking onto Anna&rsquo;s, &ldquo;I thought we could talk about&hellip; everything. Compare notes on what we&rsquo;ve been going through.&rdquo; Her words were cautious, chosen carefully to bridge the growing gap between them.
Anna nodded, sitting upright, her hands neatly folded on the table. &ldquo;Yes, I think that&rsquo;s a very sensible approach,&rdquo; she agreed, her language almost scripted. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s important to understand the changes systematically.&rdquo;
&ldquo;The sickness was brutal,&rdquo; Sonia began, barely containing her anger. &ldquo;But waking up to this… this caricature of femininity, it&rsquo;s a nightmare. It&rsquo;s like being trapped in someone else&rsquo;s distorted fantasy. It&rsquo;s completely wrong, sick and depraved.&rdquo;
Anna looked down at her gloved hands, then back at Sonia, smiling with deeply rooted, unnatural happiness. &ldquo;Sonia,&rdquo; she began, steady and composed, &ldquo;I know it&rsquo;s hard for you to understand right now, but the virus has been a gift. It erased years of insecurity, reshaping my body in ways I never thought possible. I am now slender and elegant, and my skin is smooth and flawless. The imperfections I once loathed have vanished. Even my hair—it&rsquo;s grown fuller, more vibrant. I stand in front of a mirror, and the reflection I see is perfect, beautiful. For the first time, I feel truly at peace with myself.&rdquo;
She paused, her smile fixed and gentle. &ldquo;I understand that this transformation feels like an intrusion to you, but for me, it&rsquo;s been liberating. The changes have brought a sense of harmony that I never imagined I could achieve. It&rsquo;s as if the virus knew my deepest insecurities and gently erased them, leaving behind a version of myself that feels whole and confident.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s hands clenched into fists on her lap, her coffee untouched. &ldquo;Harmony? You call this manipulation harmony? They didn&rsquo;t just change our bodies, Anna. They&rsquo;re trying to erase who we are, force us into roles we never asked for!&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s smile faded slightly into a confused frown. &ldquo;But I feel peaceful. Pleasurable even. Isn&rsquo;t it better to find peace in what we&rsquo;ve become? I feel connected to something greater. The changes, they&rsquo;re enhancements, Sonia. Why fight it when we can embrace it?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Enhancements?&rdquo; Sonia&rsquo;s frustration boiled over, her voice rising despite her efforts to remain calm. &ldquo;They violated us. My body, your body—it&rsquo;s not ours anymore. And you&rsquo;re just… okay with that? With losing yourself?&rdquo;
Anna leaned forward, her voice soft, but full of conviction. &ldquo;I haven&rsquo;t lost myself. I&rsquo;ve found a version of me that fits better in this world. The world has changed, and adapting means surviving, thriving even. Can&rsquo;t you see the possibilities?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Possibilities?&rdquo; Sonia laughed bitterly. &ldquo;You mean the chance to become something we&rsquo;re not? This isn&rsquo;t adapting. It&rsquo;s submission. And I won&rsquo;t be a part of it.&rdquo;
Anna sighed, looking at Sonia with sadness. &ldquo;I wish you could see it as I do, Sonia. There&rsquo;s peace in acceptance, in fulfilling the roles we&rsquo;ve been given. Fighting it… what&rsquo;s the point if we end up alone, alienated?&rdquo;
Sonia stood up, her chair scraping against the ground. &ldquo;The point, Anna, is to remain true to ourselves. I&rsquo;d rather be alone and myself than lose who I am for a false sense of belonging. I can&rsquo;t… I won&rsquo;t just sit back and accept this.&rdquo;
Anna and Sonia at their table in the Lakeside Café.
Sonia paused, her anger simmering as she searched for another way to reach Anna. She sat down again. &ldquo;What about Elena, your mother?&rdquo; she asked, almost desperate. &ldquo;She was a beacon of independence, a brilliant scientist. She never bowed to anyone. She taught us to stand tall, to question, to be our own persons.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s reaction was immediate, but not as Sonia expected. Her smile was serene, her tone gentle, as if correcting a child&rsquo;s simple mistake. &ldquo;My mother was indeed a great scientist, but she always knew her place. She was supportive, obedient, always putting her family and her duties before her own ambitions. She embraced her femininity and taught me the importance of our roles as women.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s heart sank, a cold realization dawning on her. &ldquo;No, that&rsquo;s not… That can&rsquo;t be right. Elena fought against those stereotypes all her life. She wouldn&rsquo;t—&rdquo;
Anna interrupted, her voice still calm, &ldquo;Sonia, I remember my mother well. She always said, &lsquo;A woman&rsquo;s strength lies in her grace and obedience.&rsquo; She lived by those words. She was my role-model.&rdquo;
The revelation hit Sonia like a physical blow. Unlike herself, Anna had been changed even more than she. Not just her body, her entire personality has been reconstructed, rewritten to fit the fantasy of the unseen man who had constructed the virus. Her rising anger bubbled to the surface. She got loud. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s bullshit, Anna! They&rsquo;ve messed with your head, changed your memories. They didn&rsquo;t just change our bodies–it&rsquo;s our minds, our selves they&rsquo;ve been editing!&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression tightened. Her patience had reached its limit. &ldquo;Sonia, please, lower your voice. A woman should be seen, but never be heard. You know that very well. There&rsquo;s no need for such language or behavior. It&rsquo;s… unbecoming.&rdquo;
Sonia felt something snap within her, a fierce energy breaking free. &ldquo;Unbecoming? You think it&rsquo;s &lsquo;unbecoming&rsquo; to fight for our right to be who we are? To not have our memories, our identities, rewritten by some… some virus?&rdquo; Sonia was almost shouting now, attracting stares from nearby tables. &ldquo;I refuse to accept this. I refuse to let them take who we are!&rdquo;
Anna looked around, embarrassed by the attention they were drawing. &ldquo;Sonia, please. This isn&rsquo;t the way. We should strive to be more composed, more… feminine.&rdquo;
But Sonia was beyond hearing. &ldquo;No, Anna. I won&rsquo;t be quiet. I won&rsquo;t be &lsquo;composed.&rsquo; And I certainly won&rsquo;t let them take you, me, or anyone else without a fight.&rdquo; Her chair clattered to the ground as she stood.
&ldquo;This isn&rsquo;t over,&rdquo; Sonia declared with conviction. &ldquo;I will fight this virus, its conditioning, everything it&rsquo;s trying to do to us. And I&rsquo;ll start by remembering Elena as she truly was—a woman who would not have stood for any of this. Not for a second.&rdquo;
Without another word, Sonia turned and stormed off. Anna went home, thinking hard. Why couldn&rsquo;t Sonia see the good in their roles? Anna felt sure now, more than ever, that this was the right way. Sonia&rsquo;s resistance felt wrong to her now.

Sonia picks up Anna# After her unsettling experience at the Lakeside Café, Sonia walked through the city, trying to calm herself and trying to come up with a plan. She needed to help Anna, fight the virus, and push back this unseen enemy. In the city, she noticed the virus had not won–yet. There were plenty of… Unchanged, normal people in normal dress, not in the weird mix of Victorian-1950s time travel retrostyle the Changed preferred. She also noticed that Unchanged, including herself, got weird looks from the Changed, as if it was her who was out of line or somehow weirdly dressed.
There was quite some pressure to conform more closely to the prevailing styles. Some Changed looked at her slightly displeased or even with hostility, making remarks between themselves about her appearance, or keeping a distance as if she smelled or was dirty. Her analytical mind concluded that she needed to fit in if she wanted to function and be effective as an agent of whatever resistance she planned to build. Still, giving in and purchasing &ldquo;Changed-approved&rdquo; fashion from the local Maylee Shufoo boutique felt like losing a battle.
Now, standing in front of a mirror, she examined her reflection. She was dressed in what society deemed appropriate: a white blouse with a lacy high collar and some pleats, a pencil skirt, sensible heels, one of the atrocious hobble-slips under the dress, and her hands covered in dark, close fitting leather gloves that reached under the cuffs of her blouse. She didn&rsquo;t feel like herself, more like playing dress-up in her private horror show. Instead of a headscarf, she wore a small dark beret, rejecting the idea of the hijab, yet she knew she would blend in far better in this outfit than the day before.
Internally, Sonia was still seething, but her anger wasn&rsquo;t directed at Anna. She recognized that Anna likely couldn&rsquo;t help her condition; it wasn&rsquo;t her fault. Anna needed help, and Sonia was determined to provide it. &ldquo;This isn&rsquo;t me,&rdquo; Sonia muttered to her reflection, feeling conflicted, &ldquo;but Anna needs me. And I need her. I can&rsquo;t fight this alone. We need to reverse what the virus has done.&rdquo;
As she walked to Anna&rsquo;s apartment in the characteristically restrained stride enforced by her Yoyah Sheengzoh, Sonia pondered the profound changes the virus had inflicted, transforming vibrant, independent women into demure, obedient figures. &ldquo;How did we get here? How did Anna change so much?&rdquo; she wondered. She missed her friend, the intelligent, strong Anna who now seemed content being controlled.
It was clear that the virus had affected Sonia, her body had changed, and from what she knew and could see, likely in exactly the same way it had affected Anna. Still, her mind wasn&rsquo;t Changed the way Anna&rsquo;s and everybody else&rsquo;s mind was changed, that much was clear. She knew things she should not know: expert makeup skills, native level spoken and written Chinese, weird behavioral rules. But apparently for her this was all optional, while for Anna it formed a new reality somehow, a compulsion to act in a certain way that Sonia lacked. She was different, partially immune to whatever the virus did to the Changed.
&ldquo;But Anna can help,&rdquo; Sonia thought resolutely. &ldquo;With her expertise in genetics and her analytical mind, if anyone can find a countermeasure, it&rsquo;s her. Maybe she can fix this.&rdquo; This thought bolstered Sonia&rsquo;s resolve to continue, despite her disdain for what the virus had done.
Reaching Anna&rsquo;s floor, waddling upstairs in the ridiculous and demeaning gait the hobble slip forced on her, she steeled herself: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll wear their costumes, play their games, if that&rsquo;s what it takes. But I won&rsquo;t let the virus win. I won&rsquo;t let it take Anna completely.&rdquo; Wearing clothes she disliked was a small price to pay if it meant saving her friend.
Upon arriving, Sonia found both Anna&rsquo;s and Julien Moreau&rsquo;s doors open. Uncertain if this was an invitation or if they had left in a hurry, she hesitated. There were no signs of Anna in her apartment, no note, just indications that she had left quickly. Worried, Sonia called out, but received no answer. Then, Anna emerged from Julien&rsquo;s apartment, her approach signaled by the quick clacking of heels constrained by her narrow underskirt. Like the day before, Anna was dressed much more formal and restrictive than Sonia was used from her: A sand-colored long pencil skirt, accentuating her waist with a wide dark brown leather belt, and a creme-colored blouse with long arms. Her hair was covered by hijab that matched her skirt in color, her hands again by white satin gloves. She also held a wide straw hat with a slightly curved brim that would partially hide her face. Seeing Anna brought a wave of relief to Sonia.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m so sorry for yesterday, Anna,&rdquo; Sonia began, her tone apologetic. &ldquo;I was… out of line. I hope we can move past it. I could really use your help to navigate… all of this.&rdquo; She gestured to her clothes, indicating her efforts to fit in.
Anna spoke softly, &ldquo;Of course, Sonia. We all need support through these changes,&rdquo; her voice calm and graceful. She gestured for Sonia to follow her into Julien&rsquo;s apartment. &ldquo;Let me introduce you to Julien.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sir, this is my friend, the late Volker Becker&rsquo;s Sonia,&rdquo; Anna introduced Sonia, curtsying. &ldquo;And Sonia, this is Julien Moreau, my neighbor.&rdquo; Sonia hesitated, but eventually copied Anna&rsquo;s behavior and curtsied as well, feeling the restrictions of her blouse and skirt as she made the unfamiliar movement. Julien greeted Sonia with a nod. His comment to Anna, &ldquo;Good girl,&rdquo; seemed to make Anna unusually happy. Sonia noticed Anna closing her eyes briefly, as if savoring a wave of joy.
With Julien&rsquo;s permission (Sonia clamped down on her feelings about this), the women prepared to leave for work. Sonia, who used to be Anna&rsquo;s equal in science, now looked to Anna for guidance in proper Changed behavior. The walk, which used to be a simple commute, now felt like a challenge to Sonia in her restrictive clothing and unfamiliar heels. She asked about Julien, &ldquo;Anna, can you tell me about Julien and what happened between you two?&rdquo;
Anna replied, &ldquo;Julien is single, and a good match for me. I cleaned his place and made him breakfast.&rdquo;
Sonia was taken aback by Anna&rsquo;s casual acceptance of her role. She pressed, &ldquo;Oh… okay. And what would the Anna I knew before have thought about this… about serving him because he ordered you to?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s response was matter-of-fact. &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t matter. The old me was wrong and would have shamed our family. I needed to change,&rdquo; she said calmly. &ldquo;The virus helped me change and made the world better, it brought harmony.&rdquo;
Sonia and Anna are walking to GAIA. To appease Anna&rsquo;s sensibilities and to blend in, Sonia dresses &ldquo;properly&rdquo;.
&ldquo;You are aware that the virus changed you and conditioned you, right?&rdquo; Sonia asked, astonished, now realizing that Anna apparently held two contradictory truths in her mind.
&ldquo;I do. But I also have always been like this. I remember growing up like this, with the values I now hold dear. I also remember a different past, but I don&rsquo;t like to. It makes me…&rdquo; Anna hesitated. &ldquo;It makes me deeply ashamed that I might have been like that, and I prefer my other past. Being like that might have brought shame upon my family.&rdquo;
Sonia struggled to reconcile this. She was clearly different from Anna and the other women she observed. She understood how things &ldquo;should be&rdquo; and how to &ldquo;behave like a proper woman,&rdquo; but she rejected this norm. Unlike Anna, who seemed compelled to conform, Sonia could resist. She remembered dreaming absurd dreams, but they had no lasting effect on her. For Anna, it appeared different. Sonia had the new skills, just like Anna did. She could do things she could not do before, doing makeup, understanding Chinese script, and she knew proper behavior and expectations. She was like Anna in this matter.
Yet, unlike Anna, Sonia was certain she had only one past and lacked the compulsions and conditioning that controlled Anna. Anna&rsquo;s acceptance of her changed self made Sonia feel uneasy. It was clear her friend had changed profoundly; they used to think alike, but now they were worlds apart.
Sonia slowed her pace, trying to comprehend how the Anna beside her had changed so dramatically. The virus had altered not just Anna&rsquo;s appearance but her beliefs and identity as well.

Anna and Julien# While Sonia was in Shanghai, Anna became increasingly involved in Julien&rsquo;s life, drawn in by the urges instilled by her viral conditioning. She woke up early, the dawn&rsquo;s light barely filtering through her curtains when her alarm sounded. Today was significant; Julien had summoned her to his apartment, and she would, of course, comply. Obedience was no longer just a duty; it had become her delight, a deep-seated pleasure that had become second nature. She selected her attire with care, aiming to impress Julien and appear desirable and attractive.
First, she slipped into her stockings, fastened with garters to a girdle that sculpted her waist. Next came her heels, a moderate seven centimeters high, enough to alter her posture and remind her of the femininity she was to project and embrace.
The restrictive Yoyah Sheengzoh posed a greater challenge; it was stiff and designed to limit her stride, serving as a constant reminder of her place and purpose. Above the waistline, she chose a nude longline bra that sculpted her figure and accentuated her virally augmented assets, covered by a cream and dark blue striped Victorian blouse. Its high collar and intricate lace reflected the modesty and elegance expected of her, the slight pressure of the collar encouraging her to hold her head high and move with grace and deliberation. The skirt, also dark blue, followed. It was poofed out by a petticoat, A-line and falling gracefully over her hobble slip, completing the silhouette of propriety and demure femininity.
Her makeup was applied with precision, enhancing her features but never overshadowing the natural beauty deemed appropriate. A cream hijab was the final adornment, covering her hair completely as required.
As she dressed, Anna felt a surge of anticipation. She was making herself attractive for Julien as well as for herself. This was who she was now, and there was profound satisfaction in that acceptance. She imagined his approval, the possibility of becoming &ldquo;Julien Moreau&rsquo;s Anna,&rdquo; and it filled her with a sense of purpose.
Arriving at his apartment, Anna used the key Julien had given her to let herself in. She moved quietly to his bedroom, taking short steps enforced by her underskirt, displaying the learned grace she had acquired since the Change. Gently waking him, she saw his eyes open, a hint of surprise followed by satisfaction at her presence.
&ldquo;Thank you, Miss Anna,&rdquo; Julien said, &ldquo;and a good morning to you. Good girl.&rdquo; A wave of pleasure washed through Anna as he spoke. &ldquo;Good morning, Sir,&rdquo; Anna replied with a smile, &ldquo;your breakfast will be ready soon, if that is convenient.&rdquo; She waited, standing next to his bed with folded hands, until he acknowledged her. &ldquo;Thank you again, Miss Anna. You may begin with that now.&rdquo; Only then did she curtsy and proceed to the kitchen.
Preparing breakfast was a joyous task for Anna. She had familiarized herself with Julien&rsquo;s kitchen, knowing exactly where everything was, and performed her role efficiently. From cracking eggs to brewing coffee, each act of service was performed with love. She meticulously set the table, ensuring everything was perfect for Julien, then waited as she remembered her mother teaching her the proper pose, standing at attention, hands folded, gaze lowered, ready to be commanded.
After Julien dressed, he joined Anna at the table she had carefully set. &ldquo;This looks delicious, Miss Anna,&rdquo; he said, smiling at her. &ldquo;You may serve now.&rdquo;
Anna had been waiting for this moment. She sprang into action, serving the eggs and pancakes she had prepared, pouring his coffee just the way he liked it. &ldquo;Is everything to your satisfaction, Sir?&rdquo; she asked softly.
&ldquo;It is perfect, Miss Anna,&rdquo; Julien replied with approval. &ldquo;Good girl, well done.&rdquo;
A wave of pleasure washed over Anna at his words. She felt it course through her, from her head down to her fingertips and toes, converging in a delightful warmth in her core. It made her feel both weak and deeply aroused. &ldquo;You may bring a cushion and kneel here, Miss Anna,&rdquo; Julien instructed, pointing to the spot at his feet.
Anna complied, bringing her cushion and kneeling on it gratefully. She positioned herself facing him, hands folded in her lap, gaze lowered. She made sure to sit upright, chest out, hips aligned, presenting herself attractively as she had been taught.
As Julien began to feed her, he was mindful of her preferences, choosing her favorite foods. Anna had always loved simple, wholesome meals—scrambled eggs with a pinch of salt and pepper, slices of ripe avocado, and whole-grain toast lightly buttered. Julien made sure each bite was to her liking, a silent show of his care and attention.
Anna received each offering with gratitude, feeling pride and satisfaction. Being fed by Julien, being under his care, was a deeply profound experience for her. Their ritual was not just about the food, it also celebrated their connection and the dynamics of their roles.
When the meal was over, Julien broke the comfortable silence. &ldquo;Miss Anna, please clean up and then proceed with your duties at GAIA.&rdquo;
Anna rose gracefully, her movements slightly hindered by her dress but carried out with practiced ease. &ldquo;Thank you, Sir, for allowing me to serve,&rdquo; she replied, her voice filled with genuine warmth and gratitude. She cleared the table and loaded the dishwasher quickly and efficiently, her mind wandering as she reflected on the morning&rsquo;s interactions and fantasized about being &ldquo;Julien Moreau&rsquo;s Anna.&rdquo; Then, she grabbed her gloves and purse and hurried to work.
As evening descended, Anna found herself once again in Julien&rsquo;s kitchen, preparing dinner. The menu was simple yet elegant: savory roast chicken seasoned with fresh herbs, accompanied by roasted vegetables and garlic mashed potatoes. When dinner was ready, Anna served Julien with grace, presenting each dish with pride, then knelt on her cushion, waiting to be fed.
As he tasted the first bite, his face lit up with satisfaction, and he uttered the words she had come to cherish: &ldquo;Good girl.&rdquo; The praise sent a wave of joy through Anna, her conditioning responding to the approval with a deep sense of fulfillment. She closed her eyes for a moment, savoring the sensation, the tangible reward for her efforts and obedience.
After sampling the first bite and approving it, Julien made it a point to first feed Anna before allowing himself his portion, making sure she got the best pieces. Anna assumed the pose she had found now almost familiar, waiting for the next bite, taking it, and then slowly savoring it. What had initially felt a bit awkward had, over time, become an intimate ritual between them, building an unexpectedly close bond.
As dinner concluded and Anna began to clear the table, Julien&rsquo;s voice carried a note of genuine appreciation. &ldquo;Miss Anna, you have outdone yourself tonight. Thank you for this wonderful meal.&rdquo; Anna responded with her usual mix of humility and pride. &ldquo;Thank you, Sir, for the opportunity to serve. It is my pleasure to do so.&rdquo;
Despite enjoying Julien&rsquo;s praise, Anna longed for more. She dreamed of becoming &ldquo;Julien Moreau&rsquo;s Anna,&rdquo; but her conditioning prevented her from expressing these feelings unless prompted. Instead, she focused on being the best she could be, hoping Julien would recognize her value and eventually ask her father to give her away to him.
One evening, as they sat together in quiet comfort, Anna knelt gracefully beside Julien&rsquo;s chair. Her heart raced as she decided to voice a question that had been on her mind. &ldquo;Sir,&rdquo; she began softly, &ldquo;do you find my style pleasing?&rdquo;
Julien looked at her, a thoughtful smile playing on his lips. &ldquo;Miss Anna,&rdquo; he said warmly, &ldquo;your style is always lovely. You have a way of dressing that shows your grace and elegance. But I&rsquo;m curious—are there any changes you&rsquo;re considering?&rdquo;
With his permission to speak, Anna felt a flutter of excitement. &ldquo;Sir,&rdquo; she said carefully, &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve noticed Maylee Shufoo has some new corsets and blouses. They&rsquo;re quite elegant. Would it please you if I included them in my wardrobe?&rdquo;
Julien&rsquo;s eyes lit up with interest. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve seen those, Miss Anna. A corset can add such elegance to a woman&rsquo;s form. It&rsquo;s quite remarkable.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s heart skipped a beat, thrilled by his reaction. &ldquo;May I explore these options further, sir?&rdquo; she asked, her voice hopeful.
&ldquo;Absolutely, Miss Anna,&rdquo; Julien replied, a note of approval in his voice. &ldquo;Your manners and taste are impeccable. Good girl.&rdquo;
Anna felt warmth spread through her at his praise, a glow that filled her with happiness. &ldquo;Thank you, sir. Your guidance means everything to me. I promise to choose outfits that reflect the dignity and respect you inspire.&rdquo;
Feeling ready and excited, Anna headed to Maylee Shufoo the next day. She was eager to see Lily, the friendly saleswoman who had helped her before. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m here to explore corsets as the new foundation of my appearance,&rdquo; Anna said with a smile. &ldquo;Julien encouraged me, and I want to make our next meeting special.&rdquo;
Lily smiled warmly. &ldquo;Of course, Anna. We have just the thing to make you unforgettable. Let&rsquo;s start with a comfortable night corset, then explore the more dramatic options.&rdquo;
Anna nodded, trusting Lily&rsquo;s expertise. &ldquo;Show me what you recommend.&rdquo;
Lily explained the importance of a good fit. &ldquo;We used to take a lot of measurements, but now we use a 3D body scanner. It ensures precision.&rdquo; She led Anna to the scanner, surrounded by modesty drapes. &ldquo;Please undress, stand on the platform, and hold still.&rdquo;
Anna did as instructed, standing on the platform as the scanner&rsquo;s green lasers mapped her body. Lily guided her through various poses, both with and without heels. The result was a detailed body scan and a sheet with precise measurements.
Based on Anna&rsquo;s preferences and measurements, Lily selected a comfortable night corset and two dramatic S-line corsets. Anna&rsquo;s eyes widened as she examined the posture corsets; they looked impressive yet efficient.
&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t be fooled by the design and lace,&rdquo; Lily said. &ldquo;These are designed to be effective. This one, with integrated shoulder braces, will dramatically accentuate your figure. It&rsquo;s designed for maximum impact, enhancing your posture to present you in the most flattering light.&rdquo;
Lily assisted Anna into a changing cabin, carefully guiding her into the elegant embrace of a &ldquo;dress to impress&rdquo; corset. As the laces tightened, Anna felt her posture align, the corset molding her body into a predetermined shape. The sensation was intense, and Anna felt her breath catch.
&ldquo;It&rsquo;s… incredibly tight. I can hardly breathe,&rdquo; Anna managed, a note of alarm in her voice.
Lily stopped immediately. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s not right. It shouldn&rsquo;t impede your breathing or hurt. If it does, it&rsquo;s not a good fit and needs to be changed or swapped for a different model.&rdquo;
Anna rechecked herself and gestured to Lily to proceed, but slowly. Lily reminded her: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s a thing to get used to, Anna. Remember, elegance comes with a certain level of restraint. The posture it encourages—bottom out, chest forward—demands attention but also symbolizes a need for support.&rdquo;
Anna gazed at her reflection, struck by the dramatic S-curve the corset gave her figure. The rigid structure and shoulder braces forced her into a pose that was both vulnerable and commanding. &ldquo;I look… different,&rdquo; she murmured. &ldquo;Powerful, yet delicate.&rdquo;
Lily smiled approvingly. &ldquo;Exactly, Anna. These corsets transform not just your appearance, but also how you&rsquo;re perceived. Julien will be captivated. It&rsquo;s a delicate dance of visibility and vulnerability.&rdquo;
Anna took a deep breath, processing her feelings. &ldquo;I feel like I&rsquo;m on display, but there&rsquo;s a strength in this helplessness. It&rsquo;s like the corset is a shield as much as it is an adornment.&rdquo;
Lily nodded. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s right. Wearing a corset like this is a statement. It&rsquo;s about creating a dynamic where you&rsquo;re both the focus and the orchestrator. The corset is confining, but it also gives you power over your shape and influences your interactions. Every movement, every pose—you&rsquo;re crafting the narrative you want to present.&rdquo;
Anna felt a surge of excitement and anticipation. The corset was demanding, but it also granted her a new form of influence and control. &ldquo;Thank you, Lily,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;I feel more ready than ever to present myself to Julien. This corset is more than a garment; it&rsquo;s a tool to show myself at my best.&rdquo;
Lily smiled. &ldquo;You&rsquo;re welcome, Anna. Remember, embracing these corsets means taking control of your shape and poise. Enjoy the journey, and let the corsets work their magic.&rdquo;
Lily led Anna to the Gowbao Yoohoo blouses, highlighting their unique features. &ldquo;These blouses make as much of a statement as the corset,&rdquo; Lily explained, holding up a Hanfu-style blouse adorned with intricate patterns reminiscent of Chinese art. The cream-colored silk felt luxurious under Anna&rsquo;s fingers, the detailed craftsmanship evident in every stitch.
The blouse was tailored to fit over the corset, with a row of intricately patterned Pankou knots fastening the front. The sleeves were bell-shaped, made from partly transparent gauze, and ended in long, close-fitting cuffs that covered half the lower arm, secured with more Pankou knots.
As Lily helped Anna into the blouse, the complexity of its design became clear. The multitude of small buttons required Lily&rsquo;s skilled hands to fasten. Once dressed, Anna couldn&rsquo;t undress herself, especially not with gloves on. The high, reinforced collar acted like a neck corset, pressing gently against Anna&rsquo;s chin and urging her head into an elevated, dignified position. Her gaze naturally lowered, the blouse dictating her posture with an authority she hadn&rsquo;t expected.
&ldquo;The Gowbao Yoohoo is designed for those who understand the power of appearance,&rdquo; Lily said, watching Anna adjust to the constraints. &ldquo;Its structure enhances not just your figure, but also your demeanor.&rdquo;
Anna stood before the mirror, transformed. The S-line corset and Gowbao Yoohoo blouse created a vision of refined elegance, combining the corset&rsquo;s enforced posture with the blouse&rsquo;s head-up, gaze-down requirement. She felt like a character from a bygone era, imbued with grace and mystery.
Her reflection captivated her. The restrictions of her attire, rather than feeling cumbersome, now seemed to elevate her. &ldquo;This is how I want Julien to see me,&rdquo; she thought, a surge of confidence blooming within. &ldquo;Dressed to impress, indeed.&rdquo; She chose two more blouses—one in a Victorian aesthetic and another that blended Chinese and Victorian designs in a modern, daring style.
Keeping her new garments on, Anna stepped out of the boutique to explore her new posture and movement. Every step and gesture required careful planning and execution. Her heels and underskirt constrained her stride, while the collar in her blouse forced her to maintain a specific pose—head held high, face slightly tilted downward. Any deviation from this pose resulted in a painful reminder from the collar. The shoulder braces and corset ensured she remained upright, preventing any slouching or relaxation. Each movement was deliberate, every stance carefully held, embodying the elegance and control her attire demanded.
Anna paused in front of a shop window, struck by her reflection. The corset cinched her waist, accentuating her hips and pushing her chest forward, creating a dramatic S-line silhouette. She was more restricted than ever, but she exuded a blend of power and grace that commanded attention. Her carefully crafted persona projected elegance and authority, with an undercurrent of vulnerability that drew the eye.
This realization empowered her. In the world of the Changed, men and women lived in distinct roles, and Anna&rsquo;s attire served as a non-verbal cue to the men around her. She was the queen, commanding presence and care, while they were the knights, pledged to serve and protect. This dynamic felt right, embodying the harmonious balance of power and dependency that their society upheld.
As Anna walked home, her rapid, staccato steps dictated by her heels and skirts, she naturally fell into her role. The glances and assistance she received confirmed that her attire was working as intended. She was a queen in her realm, where beauty and strength, vulnerability and power coexisted, guided by the unspoken rules of their society.
Her new way of dressing forced her to present herself in a manner that Julien would appreciate— &ldquo;a very advantageous cut, and easy on the eyes.&rdquo; Wearing a corset and the Gowbao Yoohoo blouse made it necessary for Julien to help her dress and undress, an act of intimacy and practicality.
&ldquo;Sir, I must inform you that my new attire, particularly the corset and Gowbao Yoohoo blouse, necessitates assistance to put on and remove. Would you find it bothersome to offer me your aid, in the absence of another suitable person?&rdquo;
Julien smiled, delighted to take on his role. &ldquo;Miss Anna, it would be my honor to assist you. Your dedication to these forms of dress, your willingness to embrace such refined elegance, is quite commendable.&rdquo;
Dressing and undressing her became their morning and evening ritual. Lacing her into the corset, fixing the shoulder braces, closing the myriad of buttons at the arms, and tightening the neck corset-like collar of the blouse was an act of intimacy. Julien locked her into her armor for the day, and she would be protected, but also stuck until he released her in the evening. She would also be stuck in the enforced pose, unable to slouch, or to move her head much.
Each task, at home for Julien or at work, became an endeavor that required first planning and then careful execution. She could still function, mostly, but only with planned and forced grace and within the limits her restraints set her.
Their evenings, when Julien would gently unbutton the blouse and unlace the corset, became moments of deep connection. These were times when Julien could see the physical marks of the day&rsquo;s restrictions on Anna&rsquo;s body, a reminder of her dedication. And for Anna, being released from the day&rsquo;s confines by Julien&rsquo;s hands was a deeply intimate experience, one that reaffirmed her choice to fully immerse herself in the demands of her dress, and in turn, her relationship with Julien. Julien was enchanted. Each &ldquo;Good Girl&rdquo; from him affirmed Anna&rsquo;s commitment to her new role.

Maid in Training# Sonia spotted a job listing from &ldquo;Harmony Household Staffing&rdquo; that seemed like a chance to get into Dr. Cheng&rsquo;s home. To make it happen, she reached out to Jiang Han, also known as &ldquo;The Broker,&rdquo; for help. They met at a busy café, where the sound of clinking dishes and low chatter offered some privacy. Jiang sat calmly, while his assistant, Mei Lin, watched everything with sharp eyes.
&ldquo;Jiang, I need to get into Dr. Cheng&rsquo;s household. It&rsquo;s the only way to get closer to the truth,&rdquo; Sonia said, her voice steady.
Jiang nodded, his face giving nothing away. &ldquo;Cheng&rsquo;s household is strict. Getting you in won&rsquo;t be easy. We&rsquo;ll have to create a background that holds up. It&rsquo;ll cost more this time. Ten thousand, upfront.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s eyes narrowed slightly, but she expected the steep price. &ldquo;Done,&rdquo; she replied without hesitation. &ldquo;What exactly will you do?&rdquo;
&ldquo;I&rsquo;ll set up fake references from high-profile households, craft a work history that fits their standards, and influence the butler&rsquo;s decision. Mei Lin will brief you on what you need to know,&rdquo; Jiang explained, nodding to his assistant.
Mei Lin leaned in, her voice clear. &ldquo;As a maid, you&rsquo;ll follow strict rules. Your appearance, behavior, and speech must fit the household&rsquo;s standards. You&rsquo;ll wear a uniform, maintain perfect posture, and perform duties precisely.&rdquo;
She continued, &ldquo;Cheng&rsquo;s staff have specific grooming rules. You’ll need to wear your hair up, covered, with gloves and proper footwear. Every detail matters, from serving tea to how you walk and talk. You&rsquo;ll be watched closely by everyone.&rdquo;
Sonia listened carefully, understanding the challenge. It was clear that Dr. Cheng specifically looked for Changed as maids. The life Mei Lin described was far from her own, but she knew it was necessary. &ldquo;How long do I have to prepare?&rdquo;
&ldquo;You have two weeks before the interview. We&rsquo;ll make sure you&rsquo;re ready. Mei Lin will oversee your training, from walking to setting tables,&rdquo; Jiang assured her.
Sonia nodded, feeling the dread of the upcoming training but determined to succeed. &ldquo;Thank you, Jiang, Mei Lin. I&rsquo;ll follow your lead.&rdquo;
As they stood up, Jiang gave her a final reminder. &ldquo;Sonia, once you&rsquo;re in, it&rsquo;s up to you. We can get you ready, but inside that house, it&rsquo;s all on you.&rdquo;
Under Mei Lin&rsquo;s strict guidance, Sonia&rsquo;s training to become a maid in Dr. Cheng&rsquo;s household began right away. Mei Lin turned her own home into a tough training ground, taking on the role of a harsh mistress to prepare Sonia for the challenges ahead.
Each morning started with roll call at precisely six o&rsquo;clock. Sonia, still getting used to the tight corset and restrictive clothes, stood at attention while Mei Lin inspected her outfit, makeup, and posture. &ldquo;The corset isn&rsquo;t tight enough, Su ni ya,&rdquo; Mei Lin said, using Chinese phonemes for Sonia&rsquo;s name. &ldquo;You must be more graceful, more constrained. This is how they expect you to look,&rdquo; she said, pulling the laces tighter.
Throughout the day, Mei Lin maintained strict discipline. Sonia was either constantly working—polishing, cleaning, arranging—or standing at attention, practicing a posture of silent alertness. &ldquo;A maid either works or stands at attention,&rdquo; Mei Lin repeated often, making sure Sonia internalized this.
Mei Lin taught Sonia to focus always on her master or her master&rsquo;s guests. &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t let your mind wander. You exist only to serve. There is nothing but service on your mind. Anticipate their needs—a word, a wave, or a fingersnap,&rdquo; Mei Lin instructed. Sonia learned to curtsy at all times, keeping her eyes lowered, and speaking only when spoken to. &ldquo;Only speak about what was asked,&rdquo; Mei Lin corrected, &ldquo;never tell, always suggest, and only when asked.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s days were a relentless test of endurance and submission. One afternoon, she was arranging a floral centerpiece in the main hall—a task testing her precision and patience. The room smelled of fresh lilies and roses, each bloom placed with care. Mei Lin watched her every move with sharp eyes.
&ldquo;Stop,&rdquo; Mei Lin commanded suddenly. Sonia froze, hands poised over the flowers. &ldquo;Your posture is slackening. Remember, a maid&rsquo;s bearing reflects her respect. Stand straighter—shoulders back, chin up!&rdquo;
With a sinking heart, Sonia adjusted her posture, feeling the corset force her spine into a rigid line. Mei Lin circled her like a predator, missing nothing.
&ldquo;You are too slow, Sonia. If you move like this in Dr. Cheng&rsquo;s house, you won&rsquo;t last a day,&rdquo; Mei Lin said coldly. She dismantled the arrangement Sonia had just perfected. &ldquo;Start again. Keep your attention where it belongs.&rdquo;
Sonia resumed the task, her fingers trembling slightly. The frustration of having her effort casually dismissed was hard to swallow, but Mei Lin&rsquo;s words echoed in her mind: &ldquo;There is no room for error, no space for self-pity.&rdquo;
Hours passed. Sonia&rsquo;s back ached from her posture, her hands sore from handling the delicate stems. Mei Lin was relentless, finding fault in every detail—from the angle of a petal to the spacing between blooms.
Finally, as evening approached, Mei Lin inspected her work again. After a long moment, she nodded curtly. &ldquo;Acceptable, for now. Remember, perfection is expected, not applauded.&rdquo;
Exhausted and emotionally drained, Sonia retreated to her small room in Mei Lin&rsquo;s house. She collapsed onto the bed, allowing herself a few moments to cry, grieving the independence she had given up for her mission. The tears were brief; she wiped them away with a determined hand. Steeling herself, she whispered into the dim room, &ldquo;This is necessary. I can do this. I must.&rdquo;
One quiet Sunday morning, as the first light of dawn filtered through the curtains, Sonia found herself immersed in her new reality. The morning began as always, with the methodical donning of her uniform—a symbol of her servitude and a constant reminder of her purpose.
As &ldquo;this humble maid,&rdquo; she meticulously polished the silverware in the dining room, her movements smooth and practiced. The silver gleamed under her touch, each piece inspected with the intense attention to detail that Mei Lin had drilled into her. The room was silent except for the soft clink of metal and the whisper of her starched petticoats.
&ldquo;More shine, you can do better,&rdquo; Mei Lin&rsquo;s voice suddenly cut through the quiet, startling Sonia. Without hesitation, she lowered her gaze, curtsied automatically, and responded, &ldquo;Yes, Mistress Mei Lin. This humble maid will improve.&rdquo; Her voice was meek, stripped of any hint of her former assertiveness. She felt her identity melding into the role, her past self a distant shadow.
Hours passed, each task flowing into the next—dusting the bookshelves, arranging the flowers—all executed with a precision that would have been unthinkable a week earlier. Sonia no longer saw these actions as chores but as parts of a dance she was compelled to perform, each step dictated by conformity and the need to please. The mantra of obedience had become a comforting rhythm, a guiding force that felt necessary and oddly satisfying.
Later, as she prepared tea for Mei Lin in the quiet of the afternoon, her hands moved with elegance and precision, placing the porcelain cups just so on the saucers. &ldquo;This humble maid hopes the tea is to your liking, Mistress Mei Lin,&rdquo; she said, her voice soft and devoid of the vibrant energy she once carried.
Mei Lin sampled the tea, her eyes assessing the maid before her. &ldquo;Very good. You are learning your place,&rdquo; she stated, nodding slightly in approval.
Sonia felt a flush of pride at the praise, not for herself but for &ldquo;this humble maid&rdquo; she had become. She realized with a pang of resignation mixed with pride that she was no longer acting; she was living the role. Each commendation from Mei Lin didn&rsquo;t just reinforce her disguise; it anchored her deeper into this new existence.
As the day waned and she prepared for evening duties, Sonia caught her reflection in the mirror. The maid looking back was composed, serene, and utterly transformed. &ldquo;This humble maid is ready to serve,&rdquo; she whispered to her reflection, fully embracing the depths of her transformation, necessary for the mission but transformative to her very core.

Return from Shanghai# Sonia&rsquo;s plane touched down in Geneva as the sun set, casting a golden glow over the city that almost seemed welcoming. The familiar landscape did little to ease the tension that had built during her flight. &ldquo;Home at last,&rdquo; she muttered, dragging her minimal luggage through the arrivals gate. The air felt different—not just the typical chill of Geneva evenings, but something more stifling, a change in the atmosphere that made her uneasy.
After a restless night trying to shake off jet lag in her small apartment, Sonia&rsquo;s mind buzzed with thoughts of her next steps. &ldquo;I need to see Anna. She needs to know everything,&rdquo; she thought, fingers hesitating over her phone before dialing Anna&rsquo;s number. The call was brief; they agreed to meet at the Lakeside Café, a spot that held memories of discussions that now seemed from another lifetime.
Sonia had been away for almost two months, and approaching the Lakeside Café, she noticed the changes right away. The quaint charm of the café seemed overshadowed by the patrons. Changed women and men were now the majority, moving with an eerie uniformity, despite the varied styles and colors of their dress. Women, tightly corseted and impeccably dressed, showcased the latest in Changed fashion—restraint taken to a new level of public acceptance. &ldquo;This isn&rsquo;t the Geneva I left,&rdquo; Sonia whispered, her heart sinking as she scanned the crowd for Anna.
Finding Anna proved more difficult than expected; her friend was almost unrecognizable, transformed not just by the corset but by the aura she now exuded. Anna sat alone, her posture perfectly rigid, dressed in an elaborate Victorian-inspired outfit that announced her acceptance of her Changed status. Her hair was neatly styled under a stylish hat, her expression serene yet distant.
As Sonia approached, her heart ached with sorrow and anger. &ldquo;How can she just sit there, looking so&hellip; content? Doesn&rsquo;t she realize?&rdquo; she thought, her steps faltering. &ldquo;Of course, she does,&rdquo; Sonia answered herself. &ldquo;She knows she&rsquo;s been altered by the virus, yet it makes her accept her Change.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Anna,&rdquo; Sonia greeted, her voice catching as she sat down. Anna smiled warmly, but her behavior showed the formal restraint typical of the Changed. &ldquo;You look&hellip; different,&rdquo; Sonia said, struggling to hold back the emotions stirring within her.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sonia. It&rsquo;s good to see you,&rdquo; Anna responded in the polite tone of the Changed, yet with a genuine smile for her friend. &ldquo;You&rsquo;ve been away for some time. How was your trip?&rdquo;
&ldquo;It was&hellip; enlightening,&rdquo; Sonia replied, her mind racing with how to explain her findings without overwhelming Anna or revealing her own turmoil. &ldquo;I discovered a lot, Anna. Things that&hellip; we need to discuss. About the Change, about what it&rsquo;s really doing to us.&rdquo;
Anna nodded, her expression unchanged. &ldquo;I look forward to hearing about it,&rdquo; she said, signaling the waiter with a grace that was both flawless and unsettling. Sonia sought a way to share her news, organizing her thoughts from the past two months. &ldquo;This isn&rsquo;t just about catching up, Anna. It&rsquo;s about understanding what&rsquo;s happened to us, to you. And it&rsquo;s about figuring out where we go from here.&rdquo; The information she carried burned inside her, and she needed to share it with Anna, hoping to use Anna&rsquo;s deep knowledge of genetics to help decode the virus.
But before Sonia could start to explain, Anna shared her own news. &ldquo;Sonia, I have something wonderful to tell you,&rdquo; Anna began, her voice filled with joy. &ldquo;Julien and I are having my giving-away ceremony next weekend. It&rsquo;s at The Jade Garden, that beautiful tea house just north of Geneva. I would love for you to be there.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s heart sank slightly, but she managed a smile. &ldquo;Anna, that sounds lovely. Can you tell me more about the ceremony?&rdquo;
Anna clasped her hands, excitement shining in her eyes. &ldquo;Of course! The giving-away is a beautiful tradition. It&rsquo;s when I&rsquo;m formally transferred from being under my father&rsquo;s guidance to Julien&rsquo;s. As a woman, I move from obeying my father to my husband. It symbolizes the continuity and respect for family values that we cherish so much now.&rdquo;
Sonia listened, her thoughts swirling. She really believes in this, Sonia thought grimly. She sees it as harmony, not subjugation.
Anna continued, &ldquo;The ceremony will be simple but meaningful. We&rsquo;ll start with a procession in the garden, followed by the formal giving away by my father. Then Julien and I will lead a tea ceremony. It&rsquo;s a symbol of our new life together, sharing and serving each other.&rdquo;
&ldquo;The Jade Garden is the perfect setting,&rdquo; Anna added, her eyes dreamy. &ldquo;Its tranquility and beauty reflect the harmony and peace I now feel. Julien and I chose it because it feels like a natural extension of our relationship—serene and grounded in tradition.&rdquo;
Sonia felt a pang of sadness seeing her friend so transformed by the Change. She struggled internally, her mission clashing with the desire to support her friend. She&rsquo;s happy, but at what cost? Sonia wondered silently.
Anna seemed to sense Sonia&rsquo;s hesitation. &ldquo;Sonia, I know this is a lot to take in, especially with everything you&rsquo;ve been through. But being part of this ceremony would mean the world to me. You&rsquo;ve always been such a dear friend, and I hope to share this important moment with you.&rdquo;
Sonia nodded slowly, her resolve firming even as she agreed. &ldquo;Anna, I wouldn&rsquo;t miss it for the world,&rdquo; she assured her friend, masking her turmoil with a supportive smile. I need to be there, not just for her, but to understand how deep this goes and to find a way to help her, she thought resolutely.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sonia! It really means so much to have your blessing,&rdquo; Anna beamed, unaware of Sonia&rsquo;s internal conflict.
Sonia took a deep breath, readying herself to share her burden. Across from her, Anna waited, calm and expectant. The bustle of the Lakeside Café faded into the background as Sonia began to speak.
&ldquo;Anna, while I was away, I wasn&rsquo;t just gathering data for academic purposes. I was investigating the Change, trying to understand its origins and implications,&rdquo; Sonia said, her voice low and serious.
Anna leaned forward, interest sparking in her eyes. &ldquo;What did you find, Sonia?&rdquo;
&ldquo;I infiltrated Dr. Cheng&rsquo;s mansion in Shanghai. He&rsquo;s one of the key figures behind the Change, the creator of LASS-VASH-2024. I managed to access his private lab and copied a lot of data—data that might help us understand how the virus works and maybe even how to counteract its effects,&rdquo; Sonia confessed, watching Anna closely.
Anna&rsquo;s eyes widened, a flicker of the woman Sonia once knew. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s incredible, Sonia. How can I help?&rdquo;
Sonia paused, the weight of her next words heavy. &ldquo;Would you help me go through the data? With your expertise, we might understand better, maybe even find a cure.&rdquo;
&ldquo;To serve in finding a cure would be a great honor,&rdquo; Anna replied, smiling serenely. &ldquo;I am happy to serve, Sonia.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s heart sank and soared at once. She really means it, Sonia thought, she&rsquo;ll help because she&rsquo;s conditioned to serve… and she thinks this is serving. &ldquo;You do mean that, don&rsquo;t you, Anna?&rdquo; Sonia asked aloud. &ldquo;You want to help?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sonia. I am happy to serve in any way that can contribute,&rdquo; Anna affirmed, her tone gentle yet firm.
Sonia realized a crucial aspect of the conditioning: It didn&rsquo;t account for the existence of immunity. Anna, conditioned to serve, would help because it aligned with her ingrained desire to be useful, especially to someone she cared about.
&ldquo;Using Anna&rsquo;s conditioning to fight what changed her feels wrong, but what choice do I have?&rdquo; Sonia thought, her internal conflict growing. This was a tool, unexpected and powerful, and while it made Sonia uncomfortable, she knew the stakes were too high to ignore any advantage.
&ldquo;Thank you, Anna. Your help could make a real difference. We&rsquo;ll start as soon as possible,&rdquo; Sonia said, forcing a smile.
As they parted ways, Sonia felt a twinge of guilt for leveraging Anna&rsquo;s conditioned loyalty, but she steeled herself with the reminder of her mission&rsquo;s importance. It&rsquo;s about saving her, too, she reassured herself.

Immune to the Virus, but not to Social Pressure# During Sonia&rsquo;s absence, Geneva&rsquo;s Changed society had shifted even more. Those who embraced their new mindsets found creative ways to adapt their work and lives to the constraints of their altered behaviors. Sonia noticed that dress codes had grown even stricter; proper attire had become crucial. The dress code she had to follow in Shanghai was now common in Geneva too, making her feel out of place without her corset and Gowbao Yoohoo.
As Sonia stepped onto the tram, she wove through the crowd, her eyes drawn to the Changed women scattered throughout the car. Dressed in their perfectly tailored outfits, they moved with an almost choreographed grace, their corsets, gowns, and accessories reflecting a beauty that was both intimidating and captivating.
Sonia, hoping for a comfortable day and dressed without a corset and Gowbao Yoohoo, immediately felt out of place. She found a vacant seat and relished the rare comfort of her less restrictive clothing. But her relief was short-lived. She quickly became the focus of a group of Changed women seated across from her. Their gazes were sharp, their whispers barely concealed, as they noted Sonia&rsquo;s casual attire.
&ldquo;Can you believe how she&rsquo;s dressed?&rdquo; one woman murmured to another, loud enough for Sonia to hear. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s almost like she doesn&rsquo;t care about upholding standards of decency.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Truly, it&rsquo;s a shame,&rdquo; another added, her tone full of disdain and pity. &ldquo;Her family must be mortified by such a display.&rdquo;
Their words, though spoken among themselves, were clearly aimed at Sonia. They reminded her of the strict societal expectations now in place. The scrutiny made Sonia shrink back in her seat, the weight of their judgment pressing down on her.
Sonia tried to lose herself in her book, but the isolation and judgment were overwhelming. Each whisper felt like a nudge, pushing her back towards the standards she had only recently escaped. &ldquo;They see me as sloppy, a disgrace. Is this really what my world has come to?&rdquo; she wondered silently.
One of the women, unable to hold back her disapproval, spoke directly to Sonia. &ldquo;Dear, wouldn&rsquo;t you be more comfortable dressing properly? It really does make a difference in how we present ourselves—and how we&rsquo;re perceived.&rdquo;
Her companion nodded, adding, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s about respect, dear. Not just for others, but for yourself and your family. You&rsquo;d do well to remember that.&rdquo;
The words stung, and Sonia felt a flush of embarrassment mixed with anger. Her instinct was to retort, to defend her clothing choices and her right to comfort. But she knew any response would likely escalate the tension. Instead, she forced a tight smile and murmured, &ldquo;Thank you for your concern.&rdquo;
Returning her gaze to her book, Sonia tried to focus, but the words blurred before her eyes. The conversation around her underscored a change she was still struggling to accept. &ldquo;Am I really the one out of step here?&rdquo; she questioned internally.
Even interactions with Anna became strained when Sonia did not conform. At the Lakeside Café, she noticed the subtle reactions from Changed patrons to her casual attire. Anna, epitomizing Changed elegance, suggested that Sonia might feel more at ease if she adhered to the new norms. She praised Sonia&rsquo;s potential to excel within Changed society, should she choose to embrace their ways. Sonia felt the pressure tighten around her like a vice, a stark reminder of the widening gap between her and her surroundings. She responded with a smile, using humor to deflect, but inside, a storm of emotions raged—grief, frustration, and a stubborn spark of rebellion.
The next day, Sonia finally gave in to the pressure, digging through her belongings to find the corset and Gobao Yoohoo blouse she had acquired in Shanghai. She dressed with a sense of resignation, feeling the familiar constriction as the corset tightly embraced her torso. The fabric of her dress flowed over the rigid structure, subtly swaying with each hindered step she took due to the tight underskirt.
As she boarded the morning tram, the difference in how she was perceived was immediate. The confined movements, dictated by the corset&rsquo;s embrace, altered her posture and gait, broadcasting a silent signal of compliance and decorum. Men on the tram noticed the change; their offers of seats were not just acts of courtesy but also tacit acknowledgments of her conformity to the expected feminine role.
One gentleman in particular stood up as she approached, gesturing to his seat with a respectful nod. &ldquo;Please, take my seat, Miss,&rdquo; he said, his voice gentle yet firm, as if granting permission rather than making a suggestion.
Sonia paused, feeling the weight of expectation in his gesture. With practiced grace, she curtsied, lowering her gaze in a display of gratitude and deference—a movement that had become second nature during her undercover assignment. &ldquo;Thank you, sir,&rdquo; she murmured.
As she perched on the edge of the offered seat, the rustle of her underskirt mingling with the soft creak of the corset, Sonia was acutely aware of the eyes upon her. The act of sitting down, once thoughtless and routine, was now a calculated maneuver. The corset enforced an upright posture, and the Yoyah Sheengzoh underskirt demanded that her legs remain closely pressed together, each aspect of her attire reinforcing her submission to the prevailing norms.
The ease with which she executed these movements startled her; they had become ingrained, automatic responses, echoes of her weeks as a housemaid in Shanghai. The familiarity of her actions on the tram left her internally conflicted. While the external acknowledgment of her compliance brought a strange sense of belonging and acceptance, it also stirred a deep unease. She was playing a role that was expected of her, yet it felt increasingly like a betrayal of her true self.
As she rode toward her destination, Sonia&rsquo;s mind was a tumult of emotions. The simple acts of accepting a seat and curtsying had highlighted how deeply the behaviors instilled in her during her time in Shanghai had taken root. She was shaken not just by how others saw her but by how naturally she had slipped into the guise of the Changed woman they expected to see.
Arriving at the Lakeside Café, Sonia noticed more women wearing masks. She watched as Changed women entered the newly installed privacy cabins, likely to unmask and enjoy a moment of respite. The sight of the masks still unsettled her—the idea of being gagged and silenced was a line she wasn&rsquo;t ready to cross.
Yet, as more women around her communicated fluently in hand-dancing, laughing and gesturing with a grace she found both beautiful and alien, Sonia felt like an outsider. She understood every gesture, but she refrained from participating, her hands resting uneasily in her lap. Each movement they made was graceful, their laughter light and genuine, communicated through the intricate ballet of hand-dancing. Sonia felt a pang of longing, a desire to share in their happiness.
&ldquo;Why do they seem so content?&rdquo; Sonia mused, watching a group of masked women re-enter the privacy cabins. &ldquo;Is it really just the virus, or is there something more? Something I&rsquo;m missing?&rdquo; Her hands twitched in her lap, mimicking the hand gestures she had learned but not used since her return.
&ldquo;For so long, I&rsquo;ve seen submission as weakness, as something imposed and unjust. But these women… they don&rsquo;t seem oppressed. They&rsquo;re not lesser; they&rsquo;re just different.&rdquo; Sonia&rsquo;s heart ached with a mix of envy and confusion. &ldquo;Could it be that there&rsquo;s strength in their serenity? Could embracing some of what they have actually bring me the peace I&rsquo;ve been searching for?&rdquo;
Her upbringing clashed with these thoughts. The ideals of independence and outspokenness that had been instilled in her now seemed strangely restrictive. &ldquo;Am I being true to myself by resisting this, or am I just clinging to what I&rsquo;ve always been told is right?&rdquo;
Sonia took a deep breath, her chest tight against the corset she had chosen that morning to blend in, yet now symbolized so much more. &ldquo;Maybe it&rsquo;s time to stop judging them. Maybe it&rsquo;s time to understand that happiness doesn&rsquo;t have one form. Perhaps, just perhaps, I could allow myself to explore this part of me&hellip; the part that might actually want to fit in with them. It doesn&rsquo;t mean I have to lose who I am&hellip; Maybe it&rsquo;s just about finding a new way to be me.&rdquo;
Later that day, Sonia found herself in a crowded market, the buzz of conversation around her almost entirely visual, a ballet of hands weaving stories in the air. She watched, feeling an invisible wall between her and the world. A vendor, a Changed woman with expressive eyes, tried to communicate an offer using fluid gestures. Sonia understood but hesitated to respond in kind, her hands stubbornly by her sides. The vendor&rsquo;s smile faded slightly, impatience flashing in her eyes. Sonia reluctantly lifted her hands, her movements unwilling, an awkward mimicry of the surrounding grace. She felt a flush of embarrassment, her heart racing as she realized her reliance on voice now marked her as other, as less. The pressure to adapt, to fold her voice away and let her hands do the talking, weighed heavily on her, like giving up.

The Vial# At the lakeside café, Sonia and Anna met, both dressed in the elegant style of the Changed. Each woman was completely covered, with a hijab, mask and gloves. Sitting bolt upright at their table, facing each other and elegantly signing back and forth they epitomized the new world Dr. Cheng had created.
Anna began, her hands crafting each sign with precision. &ldquo;Sonia, the analysis of the genome you procured has been enlightening. The virus extends far beyond just altering gender dynamics. It&rsquo;s engineered to combat climate change and the detrimental aspects of late-stage capitalism by fostering a deep sense of interconnectedness and mutual dependency among all individuals.&rdquo;
She continued, elaborating on the specific behavioral modifications encoded by the virus. &ldquo;One key adjustment encourages a significant reduction in consumption. This isn&rsquo;t merely about promoting recycling or modest consumption but about a profound shift towards minimalism and sustainability in daily life.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s hands moved gracefully, almost painting the air with her words. &ldquo;Another modification targets economic behaviors. It instills a collective drive to dismantle hyperwealth. This includes support for progressive taxation, rigorous compliance checks on corporate activities, and a communal effort to regulate industries that harm the environment.&rdquo;
Her gestures grew more animated as she delved deeper. &ldquo;Furthermore, the virus promotes a restructuring of social norms to prioritize communal well-being over individual gain. This extends into urban planning and energy usage, pushing communities to adopt green technologies and sustainable practices naturally.&rdquo;
Sonia, observing intently, responded with a casual tone that contrasted with Anna&rsquo;s formality. &ldquo;So, it&rsquo;s like Dr. Cheng is programming society to fix itself? Rewiring people to naturally fight against the very things screwing up our planet and our societies?&rdquo;
Anna nodded affirmatively, signing, &ldquo;precisely, Sonia. The virus isn&rsquo;t just changing how we interact; it&rsquo;s reshaping our priorities and our very motivations. By enhancing our interdependence, it makes us more inclined to act in ways that benefit not just individuals but the entire community.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s curiosity was piqued yet she was conflicted &ldquo;That&rsquo;s… kind of revolutionary,&rdquo; she replied, &ldquo;but also terrifying? To think that compliance isn&rsquo;t just encouraged but literally built into our DNA now.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression softened, a gentle reminder of her acceptance. &ldquo;It is a shift, Sonia, but one that could lead to a more harmonious and sustainable world. Think of it as evolution, not just at a biological level but at a societal level too. The virus challenges us to transcend our limitations and embrace a greater purpose.
&ldquo;There is more,&rdquo; Anna continued, her hands moving with deliberate precision as she explained the nuances of Sonia&rsquo;s unique condition through the sophisticated ballet of hand-dancing. &ldquo;This section here,&rdquo; she pointed on her laptop screen, detailing the genetic markers, &ldquo;explains why the virus didn&rsquo;t fully control you, Sonia. It targets a specific protein, but in your case, it attached to something similar but not identical. That&rsquo;s why you didn&rsquo;t experience the compulsions like others.
&ldquo;You possess all the knowledge imprinted by the virus, the memories and values, but without the compulsion to serve or follow. It&rsquo;s an anomaly,&rdquo; Anna summarized, her signs meticulous and her expression earnest.
After a pause, Anna offered gently, with her hands shaping each word carefully, &ldquo;I could fix you. Activate the compulsion. It will bring you peace, heal you from this… conflict you carry.&rdquo;
Sonia watched Anna&rsquo;s hands, feeling the weight of the proposition. Her response came hesitantly as her hand-dancing reflected her inner unease, her movements less fluid than Anna&rsquo;s. &ldquo;Fix me? Like I&rsquo;m broken?&rdquo; she signed, her brows furrowing slightly. &ldquo;That peace… it&rsquo;d be real peace, or just me giving up fighting? Because that&rsquo;s not peace, Anna, that&rsquo;s surrender.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s reply was patient, her hands forming each sign with empathetic precision. &ldquo;Sonia, our society values living together in harmony and creating a web of dependencies between people. With the compulsion activated, you would feel what we feel. The satisfaction of fulfilling your role, the absence of conflict within. This is not surrender. It is working with your role, not against it.&rdquo;
Sonia shook her head, her hands expressing her turmoil. &ldquo;But my role… isn&rsquo;t that something I should choose, not something a virus, or anyone else, chooses for me?&rdquo; Her gestures were rapid, a stark contrast to Anna&rsquo;s composed signing. &ldquo;I value my ability to choose, Anna. Maybe more than I value the peace you&rsquo;re talking about.&rdquo;
Anna nodded, acknowledging Sonia&rsquo;s resistance. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s your choice, Sonia. I only offer the solution because I see the struggle you endure. The virus designed our society to eliminate such internal conflicts. You are an exception, living with a foot in two worlds. I could bring you peace.&rdquo;
Sonia signed back slowly, her decision clear in her deliberate movements. &ldquo;I think… I need to keep struggling, Anna. Maybe find my own kind of peace, one that doesn&rsquo;t require me to change what fundamentally makes me, me.&rdquo;
Anna took out a small glass vial containing a clear liquid from her bag. &ldquo;Even so, I must give you this,&rdquo; she offered, handing the vial to Sonia. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s something to &lsquo;fix&rsquo; you if you ever decide you&rsquo;re ready to accept the peace of the Changed. Simply swallow it, pure or with water, and within two hours, you will be healed.&rdquo;
Sonia hesitated but took the vial and placed it in her bag. &ldquo;I… I&rsquo;ll keep it,&rdquo; she communicated through her gestures, although the idea unsettled her. They fell silent, watching as women wearing the new style of masks walked by.

Sonia does not want to be alone# Sonia resumed the conversation. &ldquo;Anna, these past months&hellip; they&rsquo;ve been transformative, sure,&rdquo; Sonia began, her hands hesitantly mirroring the signs she still felt foreign. &ldquo;There&rsquo;s a certain elegance to the rituals, to the hand-dancing. But it feels like I&rsquo;m losing a part of myself—my voice, my autonomy.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s fingers fluttered with a gentle reassurance. &ldquo;Sonia, you&rsquo;ve never truly been autonomous; none of us are. Being part of a society means we rely on each other. You&rsquo;ve simply stopped fighting that realization. Remember our previous conversation? You&rsquo;re not losing yourself; you&rsquo;re discovering how to be an active part of our collective rhythm. It&rsquo;s beautiful, isn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s gaze was pensive as she replied. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s… different. The support from men, the way they anticipate needs—it&rsquo;s a ballet of mutual support, yes. But Anna, the idea of being owned, being ‘Julien Moreau&rsquo;s Anna&rsquo;… It feels like a step back.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s hands conveyed her response with careful precision. &ldquo;In our society, Sonia, embracing our roles and accepting the leadership of our guardians—whether a father, a husband, or an eldest son—is essential. They guide us, ensuring our actions reflect well on our family, protecting the honor of our house.&rdquo;
&ldquo;But where does my individuality fit into this?&rdquo; Sonia&rsquo;s hands moved with growing confidence as she absorbed the nuances of the Changed language. &ldquo;If I&rsquo;m just living out a role, am I not sacrificing something essential about myself?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s signs were firm yet soothing. &ldquo;Our individuality is there, within the boundaries of our roles. We believe in karma, in the impact of our actions beyond just this life. Living well within our roles now contributes to harmony across lifetimes.&rdquo;
Sonia mulled over Anna&rsquo;s words, her skepticism lingering. &ldquo;So you find fulfillment by excelling within these constraints? That&rsquo;s enough for you?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; Anna signed, her expression serene. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s about contributing to something greater than ourselves. This is where I find peace. Remember, nobody exists alone. We thrive through our connections and the roles we play.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And this obedience?&rdquo; Sonia asked, her curiosity piqued. &ldquo;If I were to consider this, to step into a relationship, what should I expect? Would I have to obey without question?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes. That&rsquo;s the essence of our dynamic,&rdquo; Anna explained, her gestures patient and clear. &ldquo;A Changed man respects and cares deeply. Julien, for example, he would never misuse his role. He is as bound by the conditioning of the virus as I am. He acts with my best interests at heart, guided by the same principles that guide me.&rdquo;
Sonia sighed, her signs slowing as she considered the profound shift in perspective. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s a lot to take in. This way of living, it&rsquo;s about more than just obedience—it&rsquo;s about finding a new form of freedom within the structure, isn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;
Anna nodded, her hands showing a warm approval. &ldquo;Exactly, Sonia. It&rsquo;s about partnership and respect within the framework we&rsquo;ve embraced. It might seem like a leap of faith from the outside, but within it, there&rsquo;s a profound sense of belonging and purpose.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s hands fluttered with unease as she considered Anna&rsquo;s words. &ldquo;But what if he&rsquo;s wrong? What if he asks something of you that&rsquo;s not in your best interest?&rdquo; she signed, her movements sharp with concern.
Anna&rsquo;s response was fluid and reassuring. &ldquo;Julien and I are both conditioned to uphold our roles, Sonia. His decisions are always made with my well-being in mind, as mine are to respect and follow his lead,&rdquo; she explained, her hands painting a picture of mutual trust and responsibility. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not just about obedience; it&rsquo;s about creating a balanced interdependence that ensures both of our needs are met.&rdquo;
Reflecting on her experiences, Sonia slowly signed, &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve never felt the constraints of my attire when I&rsquo;m with others. There&rsquo;s always someone to assist, making me feel supported rather than restricted.&rdquo; She paused, a new understanding dawning on her. &ldquo;Being alone is what really makes me feel like I&rsquo;m dressed in a cage.&rdquo;
Anna nodded, her hands moving with grace. &ldquo;Exactly, Sonia. In our society, being alone is unnatural. We thrive on the connections that bind us, the roles that define our interactions. Julien&rsquo;s presence in my life isn&rsquo;t just about control; it&rsquo;s about providing the support that complements my role, ensuring I never have to face challenges alone.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s expression softened as she absorbed Anna&rsquo;s explanation, her signs becoming more thoughtful. &ldquo;So, your relationship is a reflection of how our entire society functions—always someone to assist, to support, within the roles we&rsquo;re given,&rdquo; she mused, her skepticism giving way to a cautious curiosity about the structure that defined Anna&rsquo;s life and perhaps, one day, could define hers too.
Sonia raised an eyebrow, her hands forming the question with a hesitant grace. &ldquo;And what about resistance? If someone doesn&rsquo;t fit into these neat roles?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s gestures slowed, her expression serious as she replied. &ldquo;Resistance does happen, and it leads to social pressure and, if necessary, correction. You&rsquo;ve felt it yourself, Sonia, when you didn&rsquo;t conform to our dress codes. That pressure is how we maintain order.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s curiosity deepened, her movements sharp. &ldquo;Correction? You mean, like punishment?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s reply was firm, yet gentle, emphasizing her choice of words. &ldquo;Not punishment, Sonia. Correction. It&rsquo;s about realignment and growth, not revenge. For instance, if I were to pain someone—which I wouldn&rsquo;t—Julien might correct me by causing me pain, like spanking, to specifically address that behavior. The correction matches the issue.&rdquo;
A frown crossed Sonia&rsquo;s features, communicated through her tentative signs. &ldquo;So, Julien has corrected you before?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; Anna signed back, her composure unshaken. &ldquo;Correction should directly relate to the problem. It&rsquo;s very structured. Once, I performed my household duties poorly and Julien corrected me. I had to explain what I did wrong to show I understood the problem, had to explain specifically what I would do differently in the future and thank him for the correction. After redoing my tasks, he inspected them. It was harsh and very humiliating, but that&rsquo;s the point—it&rsquo;s designed to reinforce proper behavior.&rdquo;
Sonia absorbed the information, her hands moving slowly. &ldquo;That sounds intense. Harsh and humiliating? How do you accept that?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s hands moved with conviction. &ldquo;It was difficult at first, but it&rsquo;s effective. I now pay much better attention to my duties. The humiliation helps ensure the lesson sticks. It&rsquo;s about embracing our roles fully, ensuring we don&rsquo;t deviate from the behaviors that maintain the harmony of our society.&rdquo;
Reflective, Sonia&rsquo;s gestures showed a mix of apprehension and understanding. &ldquo;I see. It&rsquo;s a comprehensive system, designed to make sure everyone plays their part correctly.&rdquo;
Anna nodded, her hand-dancing smooth and confident. &ldquo;Exactly. It may seem rigid from the outside, but it&rsquo;s what underpins our peace and order. Knowing our roles and the expectations that accompany them helps us function as a cohesive community.&rdquo;
Sonia shifted uneasily, her hands weaving a hesitant dance as she broached the subject that both intrigued and unnerved her. &ldquo;So, if I choose to be with someone here, he&rsquo;d expect obedience, and he&rsquo;d&hellip; correct me if I didn&rsquo;t comply?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s hands moved gracefully, affirming the reality within their society. &ldquo;Yes, that&rsquo;s how it works. But courtship is crucial—it&rsquo;s when you understand if there can truly be a partnership. It&rsquo;s not just about submission, Sonia. It&rsquo;s about mutual respect and finding someone who values you.&rdquo;
She continued, her gestures detailed and deliberate. &ldquo;Watch how a man treats others, especially those who serve him. Does he respect them? Is he considerate? These are signs of his true character. Men must control their impulses and interactions; this is key.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Notice the little things,&rdquo; Anna advised. &ldquo;Does he remember what you like, what you don&rsquo;t? How does he make space for you in his life? It&rsquo;s about him welcoming you into his world, not just leading it.&rdquo;
Sonia absorbed Anna&rsquo;s signs, her own hands mimicking the movements almost subconsciously. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s a lot to think about. But&hellip; there&rsquo;s something appealing about it. I guess part of me even misses the&hellip; structure, the clarity of roles I experienced while undercover.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s hands softened, conveying warmth and understanding. &ldquo;Exactly, Sonia. It&rsquo;s about finding someone who supports you, makes you feel safe, not constrained. Someone who guides but also protects. The right partner will make you feel cherished, not owned.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s hands expressed a mix of relief and newfound curiosity. &ldquo;Thank you, Anna. I&rsquo;ll consider this carefully. Maybe there&rsquo;s a way to find companionship without losing myself. Maybe… I am discovering a part of me I&rsquo;ve been too afraid to acknowledge.&rdquo;
Anna smiled through her gestures, her movements wrapping around Sonia&rsquo;s concerns with gentle reassurance. &ldquo;Take your time, Sonia. Explore these feelings. The right path will reveal itself when you&rsquo;re ready to see it.&rdquo;
As they concluded their meeting, Sonia&rsquo;s mind raced with thoughts of potential futures, the notion of engaging in a Changed courtship slowly taking root amidst her longstanding beliefs.
Coming home, Sonia stepped out of her shoes, worked her hands out of her gloves, and finally removed the mask, glad to get rid of the gag in her mouth.
Coming home, Sonia gets rid of her shoes and mask.
Sonia stashed the vial Anna had given her in the refrigerator. It was a potential key to transformation she wasn&rsquo;t ready to use. She hoped she would never need it.
Sonia moved slowly around her apartment, mulling over her conversation with Anna. She paused by the window, looking out into the quiet night. Her mind replayed recent interactions at GAIA. Among them, Henry had begun standing out more and more. He often offered her help, always seemed to be around when she needed it. As a director in finance, Henry held a respected position at GAIA, and Sonia realized she might be the target of a Changed man&rsquo;s attempt at courtship.
In the solitude of her living room, Sonia whispered to herself, &ldquo;Henry&rsquo;s been there, more than I realized. Always at the right moment, always just when I needed a hand.&rdquo; A small smile crept onto her face, the thought both comforting and intriguing. &ldquo;Could it be that he&rsquo;s been showing me he understands this&hellip; this dance of roles Anna talks about?&rdquo;
Turning back to her mirror, Sonia studied her reflection. She wasn&rsquo;t just a woman caught between two worlds anymore. She was possibly a participant in a complex ballet of social dynamics. She continued softly, &ldquo;If I&rsquo;m to consider this, to really consider Henry as&hellip; more, I need to understand what I&rsquo;m stepping into.&rdquo; Her eyes narrowed slightly in thought. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s about mutual respect and care, isn&rsquo;t it? About support within the roles we might assume.&rdquo;
Sonia sat down at her small dining table, her fingers tapping lightly on the wood. &ldquo;But I need to be clear about my boundaries. This isn&rsquo;t just about accepting help or companionship. It&rsquo;s about seeing if I can fit into this Changed world without losing who I am.&rdquo; Her voice grew firmer. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll see where Henry&rsquo;s courting can lead, cautiously engage with his gestures of support, and observe how he reacts to my responses.&rdquo;
She stood up, her decision made, feeling a blend of nervousness and resolution. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll let him guide me, but not control me. I&rsquo;ll participate but not passively. If we are to dance this dance, it will be a dance of equals.&rdquo; Sonia&rsquo;s reflection in the mirror showed a woman who was beginning to understand her place in Changed society,contemplating the possibilities of a life where she might not have to be alone, where her submissive inclinations could find a safe expression.
&ldquo;As Anna said, it&rsquo;s a negotiation, an exploration,&rdquo; Sonia concluded, her tone imbued with a newfound sense of purpose. &ldquo;Maybe, just maybe, I can find harmony in a role that doesn&rsquo;t erase me but enhances me. And perhaps Henry is the right person to explore that path with.&rdquo; Her gaze hardened with determination. She was ready to face the next day at GAIA with a clear intention to observe and interact with Henry under this new light.

A Photography Project# While Anna and Julien were making their way to London, Sonia met Henry for lunch at the quaint café &ldquo;Les Petits Chats&rdquo;, tucked away at street level in a serene corner building not far from GAIA. She was seated outside at a small french table, in the shade of a street tree.
Sonia waits for Henry at the &ldquo;Les Petits Chats.&rdquo;
Her attire for the day was a perfect blend of Changed aesthetics and everyday practicality: a teal pencil skirt that ended below her knees and a tailored waist, creating a sharp silhouette against the soft fabric of her cream blouse. She wore long, form-fitting leather gloves that reached her elbows, a necessary complement to her ensemble, and her face was hidden behind that delicate mask covered in lace she had ordered to measure recently, as she rather liked the deviation from the usual smooth dollface porcelain look that currently was so popular.
By the time Henry arrived, she had already eaten. Since the café did not have privacy boxes, she had donned a niqab after releasing her mask, passing small bites under her veil elegantly with gloved hands. Having finished eating, she had put on the mask again. Even now she still had to handle the front shield of the silencing gag with some effort. She was working it carefully behind her lips and in front of the teeth, then biting down, adjusting the fit of the mask for proper vision.
Just as she finished, adjusting to the gentle pressure of the gag filling her mouth, Henry arrived. He wore a tailored suit that he filled out in all the right ways. He greeted Sonia, who rose and curtsied for him, and seated her again. As they settled into the conversation, the topic quickly turned to Henry&rsquo;s passion—fashion as an art form.
&ldquo;Fashion, particularly the kind we cherish, is meant to be worn and not to be kept on mannequins or in chests, Sonia. It&rsquo;s an art that only truly comes alive when it interacts with people,&rdquo; Henry explained earnestly, his eyes alight with enthusiasm. &ldquo;This is especially true for restricting fashion, which is designed to alter movement and posture, to evoke a sense of helplessness and interdependence.&rdquo;
Sonia listened intently, her hands folded neatly on her lap, occasionally moving in a dance of subtle gestures that conveyed her engagement in the conversation.
